# Policies

Your app's policies, one markdown file each, managed by
[belt-policies](https://github.com/stowzilla/belt-policies).

> **Not legal advice.** belt-policies generates outlines with placeholders and
> records who agreed to what. It does not write your policies and cannot tell
> you what they must say. Have a qualified attorney write or review them.

## Files

Each `<slug>.md` starts with YAML front matter:

| Key | Meaning |
|-----|---------|
| `title` | Shown to people |
| `version` | Changes every time you publish (`belt policies publish` uses today's date) |
| `effective_date` | When it takes effect |
| `requires_acceptance` | `true` if people must agree (terms, privacy); `false` if it is only published |
| `requires_reacceptance` | `false` for a minor edit, so people who agreed to an earlier version aren't asked again |
| `draft` | `true` until the text is final; drafts are never published |
| `published_sha256` | Written by `belt policies publish`; `belt policies list` flags edits made after it |

## Workflow

```bash
belt g policy terms-of-service        # outline (draft) — see `belt policies kinds`
$EDITOR policies/terms-of-service.md  # write the real text, remove every TODO
belt policies publish terms-of-service          # version = today, writes db/migrate/…
belt policies publish terms-of-service --minor  # same, without asking people again
belt deploy dev                       # the migration copies it into policy_versions
belt policies list                    # what's here, what's draft, what changed
belt policies check                   # in CI: fails if published text was edited
```

Never edit a published version in place: the stored text is what people
agreed to. Publish a new version instead.

## API

| Route | Auth | |
|-------|------|-|
| `GET /policies` | none | Current version of every policy |
| `GET /policies/{slug}` | none | Its text (`?version=` for an older one) |
| `GET /policies/status` | signed in | What the user has and hasn't agreed to (`pending`); `?subject_id=` for a subject |
| `POST /policies/accept` | signed in | `{ "policies": [{ "policy": "terms-of-service", "version": "…" }], "via": "signup" }`, plus optional `subject_id` and `metadata` |

Send the version the person was shown. If it is no longer current, the API
answers 409 and records nothing.

## Tables

- `policy_versions` — one row per published version, with its full text and hash
- `policy_acceptances` — one row each time someone agreed: user, email, version,
  text hash, time, `via`, IP address, user agent, and the subject it was about
